From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 02:52:20 +0200
Subject: net: lantiq: mdio: make the GSWIP MDIO driver per-instance

The driver was written for one bus and held three things at file scope or
as GSW-L literals. Each one turns into a regression of the *working* LAN
bus the moment a second bus probes, which is why the cleanup lands before
the node that would trigger it.

  - ltq_gswip_mdio_ctrl_mbusy was a file-scope bool shared by every
    bus, written by a probe-time calibration that read a hardcoded PHY
    address 5. On the GSW-R bus, whose only PHY is at address 1, that
    read finds nothing, fails the 0xD565 check and sets the flag -
    retroactively switching the already-calibrated, already-working
    GSW-L bus to the MBUSY=1 convention. That convention costs corrupted
    reads and a BMCR resume that never takes, leaving a PHY stuck in
    PDOWN. Probe-order-dependent, with no diagnostic pointing at the
    second bus. The flag is now per-bus, and calibration walks the bus's
    own DT children, trying each until one answers.

  - The MDC_CFG_0 polling mask was the literal 0x003C (PEN_2..PEN_5, the
    four GSW-L GPHY addresses), written unconditionally. This polling
    unit is the only link-to-MAC coupling there is here - phylib's
    adjust_link is an empty stub - so on the GSW-R
    bus the literal would enable polling for four addresses that do not
    exist and leave the actual WAN PHY unpolled, i.e. no link->MAC
    coupling at all. The mask is now the OR of BIT(addr) over the bus's
    DT children, which reproduces 0x003C exactly on GSW-L and gives
    0x0002 on GSW-R. A PHY outside the PEN_ALL range 1..6 warns rather
    than silently losing its bit.

  - The delayed re-init work calls gsw_hw_reinit_gswl() by name -
    an ETHSW_SWRES pulse plus PCE microcode and PMAC table reload - and
    was scheduled from every probe. From a GSW-R bus it would reset the
    working LAN switch core ~31 seconds later at an unplanned moment,
    while driving MDIO master registers through the wrong aperture. There
    is no gsw_hw_reinit_gswr(). It is now scheduled from instance 0 only,
    read from a lantiq,gswip-instance property that defaults to 0 so a DT
    predating the second bus behaves identically.

The 7590 then gains gswip-mdio@1a003c10 with one ethernet-phy@1 child.

Two things about that address and that child are worth stating rather
than leaving to be rediscovered:

  - 0x1a003c10 is arithmetic, not measurement. It is the GSW-L derivation
    (base + (MDCTRL DWORD + GSW30_TOP_OFFSET) * 4) applied to the GSW-R
    base. The vendor device tree gives its equivalent node no reg at
    all, hanging the bus off the switch node instead, so this address
    has no vendor counterpart to check against. If it is wrong, the
    symptom is contained and specific: reads on this bus return 0xFFFF
    and the WAN PHY never enumerates, with the LAN bus unaffected.

  - The child is ethernet-phy@1, not @15. The "MDIO address == dp-port-id"
    invariant documented on the GSW-L bus - which a previous ascending
    wiring got wrong there - is a GSW-L coincidence: dp-port-id is an
    SoC-wide datapath id, the MDIO address belongs to the bus. The WAN
    PHY is address 1 and its port is dp-port-id 15. The comment on the
    GSW-L bus now says so.

Checked with dtc: all three boards still build. The 7560 DTB's only
change is the explicit lantiq,gswip-instance = <0>, and easy350_anywan is
untouched.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
index 62c92e26d3a9..33f5b176a40d 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm.dtsi
@@ -70,6 +70,7 @@ gphy_fw: phy-xrx500@1c003c00 {
 	gswip_mdio: gswip-mdio@1c003c10 {
 		compatible = "lantiq,gswip-mdio";
 		reg = <0x1c003c10 0x20>;
+		lantiq,gswip-instance = <0>;
 		#address-cells = <1>;
 		#size-cells = <0>;
 		status = "okay";
diff --git a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
index 6c247e7aecaf..e6ba85f127e1 100644
--- a/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
+++ b/arch/mips/boot/dts/intel-mips/seale_avm_fritz7590.dts
@@ -182,3 +182,39 @@ &gphy_fw {
 		 <&rcu_reset0 25 25>;
 	reset-names = "phy2", "phy3", "phy4", "phy5";
 };
+
+/*
+ * The second MDIO bus, on the GSW-R instance, carrying this board's WAN PHY.
+ *
+ * The aperture is the GSW-L arithmetic in seale_avm.dtsi applied to the GSW-R
+ * base: gswr_base + (MDCTRL DWORD 0x004 + GSW30_TOP_OFFSET 0xF00) * 4 =
+ * 0x1a000000 + 0x3c10. Same 0x20 span covering MDCTRL/MDREAD/MDWRITE and the
+ * two MDCCFG registers, same intentional overlap with gswr@1a000000's 0x4000
+ * reg claim, same devm_ioremap-without-request_mem_region shape that makes
+ * the overlap harmless.
+ *
+ * If the arithmetic is wrong the symptom is specific: reads on this bus
+ * return 0xFFFF and the WAN PHY never enumerates, while the LAN bus is
+ * unaffected.
+ *
+ * Everything else the driver needs is read from the child below.
+ *
+ * The PHY is at MDIO address 1. The "MDIO address == dp-port-id" rule
+ * documented on the GSW-L bus is a GSW-L coincidence and stops here: this
+ * PHY's port is dp-port-id 15, and writing ethernet-phy@15 would name an
+ * address that does not exist.
+ */
+/ {
+	gswr_mdio: gswip-mdio@1a003c10 {
+		compatible = "lantiq,gswip-mdio";
+		reg = <0x1a003c10 0x20>;
+		lantiq,gswip-instance = <1>;
+		#address-cells = <1>;
+		#size-cells = <0>;
+		status = "okay";
+
+		gphy_wan: ethernet-phy@1 {
+			reg = <0x01>;
+		};
+	};
+};
